export const lsConcepts401500: any[] = [
  { id: "ls-nama-401", title: "Vividhākārā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who has a multitude of forms. The word joins vividha, meaning manifold, with ākāra, meaning form, so the literal sense is the Goddess of countless shapes. Doctrinally this gathers every function-form described earlier: as creator She appears as the supreme Mother, as sustainer in the Viṣṇu mode, as dissolver in the Rudra mode, and as witness when all things resolve. The passage traces all such figures back to the root matrix of nature, so no single icon exhausts Her. A later name calls Her the many-formed one again, confirming the point. In contemplation, meet each circumstance as one of Her faces rather than demanding a single fixed image.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-087"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-402", title: "Vidyā'avidyāsvarūpiṇī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the form of both knowledge and ignorance. Vidyā here means liberating knowledge of the Self, while avidyā means nescience that binds one to rites and outer worship performed for reward. The source recalls the old teaching that detached ritual together with inward inquiry ripens into bliss, but release comes only when nescience dissolves into wisdom. She both grants the upward pull of clarity and permits the veiling play of delusion through Her power of seeming. Thus She stands behind the seeker and the straying one alike. In practice, offer the fruits of every act without grasping, and turn outer worship steadily inward toward silent knowing.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-087"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-403", title: "Mahākāmeśanayanakumudāhlādakaumudī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the moonlight that gladdens the water-lilies that are mahākāmeśa's eyes. The compound pictures the great Lord's eyes opening like night lotuses under the bright moon of the Kārttika season when He beholds Her. A second reading divides kumuda into ku plus mud, inferior pleasure, so worldly delights are like dim blooms She pities and redirects toward liberation through Him. Hence maternal compassion converts even lower craving into a path home. Her mere remembrance is said to lighten difficulty. In contemplation, picture every longing laid before the divine gaze, asking that pleasure be ripened into clarity rather than sorrow.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-087"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-404", title: "Bhaktahārdatamobhedabhānumadbhānusantatiḥ", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the sunbeam which dispels the darkness from the heart of Her devotees. The image is of sunrise scattering night: the heart's gloom is nescience, and Her ray is awakening wisdom. The passage stresses that She acts from motherly compassion, not from bargaining, and that even the thought of Her begins to thin ignorance. It recalls the promise that the indwelling Lord kindles the lamp of knowledge out of mercy. Darkness here means forgetting one's true ground. In practice, when confusion or heaviness gathers, turn the mind once with trust toward Her presence and let that remembrance do the clearing.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-088"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-405", title: "Śivadūtī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She for whom shiva is the messenger; She who is shiva's messenger. The name rests on the episode in which the Goddess deputed Śiva Himself to bear Her message to opposing powers, reversing the usual order of command. Grammar allows both senses: She whose envoy is Śiva, and She who goes as His envoy, underscoring Their inseparable agency. The commentary also notes that this title belongs to one of the fifteen lunar-tide goddesses of the sacred diagram. Sovereignty here expresses intimacy, not distance. In contemplation, recall that the highest power can serve love, and let pride yield to willing service in your own duties.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-088"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-406", title: "Śivārādhyā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is worshipped by shiva. Literally She is the object of Śiva's own adoration, the one He honors in meditation. The passage says that through such turning toward Her He shines as half-Śiva and half-Śakti and becomes lord of wondrous capacities, all of which flow from Her subtlest coiled energy. The opening stanza of the hymn of beauty is cited: joined with the Goddess He can create and pulse, without Her not even throb. Worship here is mutual indwelling. In practice, take the divine pair as your model: still awareness honoring living energy, and energy honoring stillness, in each sitting.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-088"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-407", title: "Śivamūrtiḥ", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She whose form is shiva Himself. Mūrti means embodied form, and Śiva means the gracious and auspicious one, so the name declares no ultimate difference between Goddess and God. The commentary hears in this the formless ground wearing a gracious face: full of grace, happiness, and protection, as an ancient hymn to the mighty guardian suggests. Read with the closing names of the thousand, it points to the union of the two as the final word. Liberation-form is also implied, since release comes through knowing. In contemplation, refuse to split stillness from power; honor the one Reality in both its quiet and its vivid aspects.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-088"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-408", title: "Śivaṅkarī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who confers prosperity (auspiciousness, happiness). She who turns Her devotees into shiva. The word joins the auspicious one with the maker, so She is the maker of welfare. The passage explains the mechanism: She clears the devotee's nescience, and as ignorance lifts, knowledge opens into Self-recognition until the worshipper shares Śiva's own nature. The hymn to the Goddess as the luck of all lucky things is recalled in support. Blessing here is ontological, not merely circumstantial. Her gift turns lack into auspicious fullness, ending the inner poverty that forgets its shining source. In practice, ask less for pleasant outcomes and more for the lifting of the inner veil, trusting that joy follows clarity.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-088"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-409", title: "Śivapriyā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is beloved of shiva. Priyā means dear one, so the literal sense is the cherished of the still Lord. The commentary stresses mutuality: He is dear to Her and She to Him, sealed by His gift of the left half of His own body to Her in their joint form. Love here is not sentiment added to metaphysics but the very bond of the Absolute with its power. Devotion thus mirrors an eternal relation. In contemplation, let your affection be steady and reciprocal: approach the divine as one who is already loved, and answer that love with faithfulness in conduct.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-089"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-410", title: "Śivaparā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is solely devoted to shiva. Parā can mean devoted to, but also higher than, and the passage develops both senses toward transcendence: She reveals and even outshines the Śiva-form as the supreme ground. It distinguishes the higher Absolute beyond qualities from its qualified appearance, arguing that the Lord grows still without His power, so She is the supreme knowing itself. Her forms as radiance, reflection, and great illusion support this reading. The series on Śiva thus crowns Her as Parā. To know Her thus is the higher knowing itself, the supreme science that crowns and completes every lesser study. In practice, move from loving form to resting in the formless source that lets every form shine.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-089"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-411", title: "Śiṣṭeṣṭā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is loved by the righteous; She who is the chosen deity of devotees; She who loves righteous people. Śiṣṭa means the disciplined who restrain the senses, know the Veda, and act by sacred rule, while iṣṭa means cherished and chosen. The commentary links Her with the same title borne by Viṣṇu and recalls the teaching that the steady knower, constant in single devotion, is most dear to the Lord and the Lord to him. Such illumination ripens across many lives. Worship must follow one's rightful discipline, not mere display. In practice, purify senses and motive, study the wise, and make steadiness itself your offering.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-089"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-412", title: "Śiṣṭapūjitā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is always worshipped by the righteous. Pūjitā means honored with worship, so the name says the disciplined knowing ones continually adore Her. The passage reads this as sequel to the last name: the wise worship only what is worthy, namely the supreme Self, so their worship proves Her ultimate stature. Adoration by the discerning is presented as evidence, not flattery. The circle closes when love and worth meet. Their steady worship becomes a compass for our own, proving the path by feet that already walk it surely. In practice, test your own devotion by its object: turn praise away from passing powers toward the one ground, and keep that worship regular and inward.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-089"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-413", title: "Aprameyā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is immeasurable by the senses. The privative a- with prameyā, the measurable, yields the immeasurable, a title also borne by Viṣṇu. The commentary argues that what lacks sound and sensible marks cannot be pictured, compared, or fixed by affirmation or denial, since every comparison limps. Yet scripture gestures toward That as self-shining witness through disciplined negation and affirmation. The thirst for basic guidance is acknowledged and deferred to the next name. Scripture therefore points through disciplined denial and affirmation together, like a finger toward the moon, confessing that the ground exceeds each grasp while guiding each step. In practice, notice each attempt to size up Reality and release it; bow before the measureless with the humility of unknowing.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-089"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-414", title: "Svaprakāśā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is self-luminous. Sva means own and prakāśā means shining, so She shines by Her own light while sun, moon, and stars borrow theirs. The passage cites the ancient image of the ground revealing its luster by itself, free of the elements and fear because nothing stands outside it, and the saying that where the Absolute shines all else follows. Her light is what makes the world visible at all. A further note joins this shining stillness with reflective power whose union manifests the cosmos. In contemplation, trace every outer brightness back to the inner light by which you know brightness itself.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-089"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-415", title: "Manovācāmagocarā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is beyond the range of mind and speech. Go-cara means field or pasture, so with mind and speech negated the name places Her outside their grazing ground. The passage extends the previous immeasurability: whatever the mind grasps is only its own intersection, and words return unsatisfied before the boundless, as the teaching on the source of speech declares. Thought handles only the familiar, while this is prior to all experience. Realization comes through a purer knowing than report. In practice, let mantra and reflection lead to the edge of silence, then rest without forcing an image or phrase.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-089"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-416", title: "Cicchaktiḥ", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the power of consciousness. Cit means stainless awareness and śakti means power, so She is awareness itself as energy. The passage sets Her within the triad of being, consciousness, and bliss that names the Absolute, stressing that only unafflicted knowing yields spiritual wakefulness. It further distinguishes the Absolute from its reflections in cosmic and individual nescience, introducing the lordly principle that governs nature while remaining untainted by the strands. An old verse hiding both knowledge and nescience in the higher ground is cited. Rest there, and the reflections settle; behind many flickering minds one steady light is seen to burn. In contemplation, separate pure witnessing from passing moods and rest as the witness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-090"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-417", title: "Cetanārūpā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is pure consciousness. Cetanā names awareness in its living, operative poise, a purer unfolding of the preceding power. The commentary describes two faces of its emergence: a gross side of name and form giving outer encounter, and a subtle side of mind, intellect, and ego giving inner experience, together shaping both objective and subjective worlds. This operative awareness is also called Her reflective mode, complementing sheer illumination. From it the whole play of experiencing arises. Outer scene and inner musing are twin mirrors of one awareness; neither mirror is the face, yet each can reflect it faithfully. In practice, watch how outer scene and inner commentary co-arise in one knowing, and relax identification with either pole.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-090"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-418", title: "Jaḍaśaktiḥ", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the māyā that has transformed itself as the power of creation. Jaḍa means non-pulsating or inanimate, so the name is the still, material potency facing the vibrant pole of pure awareness. The passage contrasts lower nature — earth, water, fire, air, ether with mind, intellect, and ego — against the higher life-principle that sustains the cosmos, citing the famous eightfold analysis. Without the veiling ground the Absolute would not stage-manage plurality, yet without awareness the ground could not shine. Both belong to the qualified Absolute. In practice, honor the body and world as Her slower rhythm, not as enemies of spirit.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-090"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-419", title: "Jaḍātmikā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is in the form of the inanimate world. The feminine ātmikā means whose very self is this, so apart from the Absolute all objective display is Her assumed shape. The passage unfolds a subject-object inquiry: if the ground were a seen thing it would perish like things, yet we gain all knowing through objects perceived by senses; thus the knower and the known must converge beyond mere discussion. Forms are granted only as gracious aids. Scripts give the formless no fixed figure. In contemplation, use each object as a pointer rather than a prison, asking who beholds, until beholder and ground are felt as one.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-090"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-420", title: "Gāyatrī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the gāyatrī mantra. The name identifies Her with the twenty-four-syllable meter praised as strongest among meters and as the word that names and shelter all things. The passage retells the story of Brahmā wedding Gāyatrī for the rite, cites the saying that among meters the Lord is this one, and unpacks the mantra's arc: sacred syllable, three world-utterances for lower, common, and higher awareness, then three lines hailing the radiant source, meditating on its splendor, and praying that it stir our thought. In practice, recite slowly at dawn and dusk, letting each line lift attention from world to light to surrender.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-090"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-421", title: "Vyāhṛtiḥ", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is in the nature of utterance; She who presides over the power of speech. Vyāhṛti means solemn utterance, here the three great calls placed after the sacred syllable that unfold it into worlds. The passage treats them as Her speech-form already unfolded in the prior mantra exposition: earthly, mid, and luminous planes, which are also grades of awareness from dim to ordinary to bright. She governs the bridge by which sound becomes cosmos. Reciters thus meet Her at the threshold of prayer. In practice, sound each utterance distinctly before the mantra, feeling voice, breath, and meaning gather into one current.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-090"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-422", title: "Sandhyā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is in the form of twilight. Sandhyā first means non-separation, the mind's union with living awareness figured as dwelling in the sun, and only secondarily dawn and dusk. The passage explains why the sun is chosen: visible sustainer of earth, its rays imaging Her manifold works while its own light is borrowed from the self-shining ground. Practice at the joints of day is said to quicken this union, ripening at last into likeness to the gracious Lord. She is also told as Brahmā's daughter turned seer-wife, and as the meeting of the three channels at the brow. In practice, meditate at twilight on non-duality dissolving seer and seen.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-090"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-423", title: "Dvijavṛndaniṣevitā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is worshipped by the twice-born. Dvija means twice-born, first of mother and second of mantra initiation, and vṛnda-niṣevitā means served by their host. The passage says only the initiated rightly bear the famous solar mantra, normally received from the father, and reads the neighboring names as the three states of waking, dream, and deep sleep. Waking is full outward awareness, dream is partial inward play, and dreamless rest merges mind in the Self to gather sustaining force, opening toward the fourth beyond. In practice, honor initiation by right recitation, and observe the three states daily to glimpse the sleepless ground.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-090"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-424", title: "Tattvāsanā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who has tattvas as Her seat; She who abides in tattva. Āsana means seat or throne, so the principles themselves form Her pedestal. The passage counts them as twenty-four with inner instruments and outer senses rooted in the five elements and their subtle essences, rising to thirty-six when the pure orders of the still and dynamic poles are added. Mind, intellect, awareness, and ego work within while senses extend without. Yet She also exceeds what enthrones Her. Enthroned thus, She commands every instrument while remaining ever free, seated on principles yet bounded by none. In contemplation, survey body, sense, and mind as layered tattvas supporting awareness, then rest as the enthroned one beyond every layer.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-091"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-425", title: "Tat", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is meant by 'That', the supreme truth, brahman. Tat is the demonstrative That, joined with the sacred syllable and being in the triple designation of the Absolute. The passage, shared with a Viṣṇu title, cites the teaching that seekers of release perform rites fixed on That without craving results. That names the purest, highest God-consciousness beyond pointing. She is that ultimate That wearing motherhood. Some versions divide these syllables differently; the tat-tvam-ayi reading is noted as uncertain in the source. Hold That before the mind in rite and rest alike, and all lesser designations quietly fall away like leaves. In practice, dedicate each act to That alone, releasing anxiety about its fruit.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-091"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-426", title: "Tvaṃ", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is referred to, by 'Thou'. Tvam is the pronoun you, here the intimate Absolute addressed face to face. The passage recalls the teaching that the ground differs from every known and even unknown object, and that release is the sudden flash joining That with I. The great dictum you are That binds this name to its neighbors into one sentence of identity. She is both the Thou invoked and the I awakened. Some versions divide these syllables differently; the tat-tvam-ayi reading is noted as uncertain in the source. In contemplation, turn address into recognition: the Thou you call upon is your own deepest self.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-091"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-427", title: "Ayī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "Oh, Mother! (The split in names of 425-427 may not be proper.). Ayi is an affectionate vocative for mother or sister, warmer with love than with formality, and it also breathes auspiciousness. The passage notes that the three syllables together compose the being-full-of-principle celebrated later in the thousand, tracing a movement from gross to subtle as the hymn ascends. Read singly, this cry melts doctrine into devotion. Some versions divide these syllables differently; the tat-tvam-ayi reading is noted as uncertain in the source. In practice, set argument aside at times and simply call to Her as Mother with tender trust.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-091"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-428", title: "Pañcakośāntarasthitā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who resides within the five sheaths. Kośa means sheath, so She dwells inside the five coverings of the embodied person: food-formed body without, vital airs, mind, intellect within the subtle frame, and bliss nearest the causal core where the soul with its karmic seed abides. The passage explains how these veils project microcosm and macrocosm, hiding the inner person. It adds the ritual reading of five goddesses at the diagram's center and the yogic reading of stages toward trancless absorption. In contemplation, peel attention inward sheath by sheath, from body to breath to mind to understanding to joy, and rest in the dweller.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-091"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-429", title: "Niḥsīmamahimā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She whose glory is limitless. Niḥsīman joins negation with boundary, and mahimā means greatness, so Her splendor has no edge. The passage grounds this in omnipresence: as the Absolute She is not contained by place, time, or measure. Wherever awareness reaches, Her majesty is already there. The brevity of the source mirrors the point — bounds fail, and commentary must fall short. No hymn can bound what hymns proclaim, for each measure of praise only uncovers a further horizon of splendor without any shore. In practice, when the mind tries to localize the sacred in one shrine, image, or mood, widen the gaze until every direction discloses the same boundless shine.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-091"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-430", title: "Nityayauvanā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is ever youthful. Nitya means perpetual and yauvanā means youth, so aging never touches Her. The passage roots this in transcendence of space and time: what generates decay stands outside Her, while She renews all cycles without growing old. Unlike embodied beauty that fades, Her freshness is the inexhaustible vigor of being itself. Devotion thus meets always a living presence, never a relic. Devotion therefore never meets decay in Her, only morning renewed; draw near and feel time loosen its grip upon hope. In contemplation, offer your own fear of age and loss to Her undimming vitality, and ask for a mind kept supple, curious, and ever beginning anew.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-091"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-431", title: "Madaśālinī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is shining in a state of inebriation or intoxication. Mada here is not dull stupor but the rapture of bliss where thought rests wholly in the Absolute. The passage pictures Her delight as shared with Her consort while She never abandons the works of creation, sustenance, and withdrawal — a living emblem of disinterested action praised in the song of duty. The next two names unfold its bodily signature. Joy and duty kiss in this name, teaching that ecstasy rightly held steadies the hands instead of dropping their needful tools. In practice, let devotion gladden without abandoning responsibility: carry an inward wine of joy into exact outward work, acting wholly yet clinging to nothing.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-091"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-432", title: "Madaghūrṇitaraktākṣī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She whose eyes are reddened, rolling with rapture and inward-looking. The compound paints eyes flushed and gently turning from bliss, the natural redness also belonging to Her symbolic color. The passage compares this with the meditator's reddened gaze after deep absorption, when gathered heat rises, and counsels water, fresh food, and the conversion of heat into healing force. Physiology thus mirrors theology. The inward roll signals attention withdrawn from externals. Read the flush as signal rather than ornament: depth has been plumbed, and the pearl of inwardness now colors the gaze. In practice, when eyes burn from strain or intensity, cool and rest the body, then redirect the saved energy toward quiet inner gazing.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-092"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-433", title: "Madapāṭalagaṇḍabhūḥ", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She whose cheeks are rosy with rapture. Pāṭala means pale red, so Her cheeks bloom with the same bliss that reddened Her eyes. The passage says steady meditators likewise take on a golden sheen as awareness detaches from outer objects and nears the self-shining light, the body reflecting inward dawn. Complexion becomes a bulletin of consciousness. Beauty here reports union, not cosmetics. As dawn pinks the clouds while the sun still hides, so inward nearness tints the outer frame before full realization breaks openly into view. In practice, value the quiet radiance that follows sincere sitting more than outer adornment, and let warmth of heart show gently in face and speech.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-092"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-434", title: "Candanadravadigdhāṅgī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She whose body is smeared with sandalwood paste. Candana means sandal and dravadigdhāṅgī means limbs anointed with its fluid, a cooling unguent in sacred use. The passage reads this simply as tempering the inner heat kindled by rapture and meditation, balancing fire with fragrance. Sense and symbol meet: coolness without, composure within. The adornment is also hospitality to devotees who behold Her. Coolness consecrated becomes compassion in act; the same heart that burns with zeal learns to soothe with sandal-soft patience toward all. In practice, after ardent effort invite cooling measures — measured breath, water, silence, and sweet conduct — so that intensity matures into steady, fragrant calm.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-092"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-435", title: "Cāmpeyakusumapriyā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is especially fond of champaka flowers. Cāmpeya names the golden michelia blossom and priyā means fond, so the literal mark is delight in this fragrant flower. The passage recalls an earlier mention of the same liking, showing continuity in Her iconography of scent and beauty. Flowers signify the devotee's freshest feeling laid at Her feet, brief yet sincere. Her fondness dignifies small offerings. Tenderness toward a blossom trains tenderness toward souls: the small, the brief, and the sweet are never despised in Her fragrant court. In practice, place one real flower or one fresh good thought before Her daily, valuing simplicity, fragrance of motive, and regularity over grandeur.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-092"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-436", title: "Kuśalā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is skillful. Kuśalā means dexterous and adept, the one who handles difficult work with ease. The passage applies this to the triple divine office of projecting, maintaining, and withdrawing the worlds without strain or confusion. Vast cosmic administration appears in Her hands as effortless craft. Skill here includes wisdom, timing, and care. Three cosmic offices move through Her fingers like shuttle through loom, weaving preservation between projection and withdrawal without one dropped thread or tangled knot. In practice, imitate that economy: divide great tasks into clean steps, attend fully to each, and let competence become worship by relieving rather than burdening others.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-093"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-437", title: "Komalākārā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is graceful in form. Komala means tender and ākārā means figure, so Her embodiment is soft, delicate, and lovely. The passage stresses gracious beauty rather than awe, the approachability of power robed in gentleness. Tenderness does not dilute sovereignty; it reveals its maternal texture. Form becomes invitation. Strength robed in softness disarms fear better than thunder; approach Her gentleness first, and awe will ripen slowly into love that obeys gladly. In contemplation, picture the divine not only as vast and fiery but as tender-limbed kindness, and let your own bearing grow gentler toward the fragile in people and things.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-093"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-438", title: "Kurukullā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the shakti, kurukullā (residing in kuruvindā ruby). Kurukullā names a fierce goddess stationed at the border of ego and awareness within the sacred diagram. The passage cites the meditation text that calls the boar-faced guardian father and this goddess the mother of offerings, since flesh, blood, and senses breed bodily craving. Her terrifying aspect warns that lust blocks ascent. Yet as Śakti She can convert desire into fuel. Her forbidding mask is mercy in armor, warning the climber where the path grows steep with craving before the fall begins. In practice, name craving plainly when it rises at the threshold of attention, and hand its energy over to steady mantra and breath.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-093"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-439", title: "Kuleśvarī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the ruler of kula (the triad of knower, the known and knowledge). Kula here means the perceptual triad, and īśvarī means sovereign, so She governs seer, seen, and seeing. The passage teaches that when the three collapse into one, She is realized: division was Her play, unity is Her reveal. Lordship means She both projects distinction and withdraws it. Epistemology turns into devotion. Where seer, seen, and seeing fuse, command and communion become one act, and Her sovereignty is tasted as intimate identity. In contemplation, observe knower, known, and knowing as they arise together, then relax the seams until only luminous knowing remains, which honor as Her.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-093"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-440", title: "Kulakuṇḍālayā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who abides in the kulakuṇḍa (the bindu at the center of the pericarp in mūlādhāra chakra). Kulakuṇḍa is the tiny hollow at the root center where the coiled power sleeps. The passage cites the verse of Her reaching Her own ground, assuming serpent form with three and a half coils in the root lotus crevice. Every ascent begins there and climbs toward the fourth state and beyond, where bliss and release flower. She is both sleeper and awakener. In practice, settle attention low at the root with slow breath, honoring the resting serpent before inviting Her gentle upward rise.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-093"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-441", title: "Kaulamārgatatparasevitā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is worshipped by those devoted to the kaula tradition. Kaula-mārga means the lineage path served with dedication, honored by its own ritual grammar. The passage distinguishes three approaches: the Vedic way of scripture, the tantric way of potent symbols sometimes misused, and the mixed way blending both. The point is fidelity to one's transmitted discipline rather than contempt for another's. Method must suit maturity. Fidelity to the received rule matters more than curiosity for another's; grind your own grain purely, and the same flour of peace will result. In practice, follow the rule you have received with sincerity and restraint, keep conduct clean, and let outer rite ripen steadily into inward attention.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-093"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-442", title: "Kumāragaṇanāthāmbā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the mother of kumāra (subrahmanya) and gaṇanātha (Gaṇapathi). Ambā means mother, so outwardly She is mother of the spear-bearing youth and the elephant-faced lord of hosts. Inwardly the passage reads ku plus surging passion with their lords as the binding ego born when spirit weds unmanifest nature, the army-chief between king and queen. Worship of the youth is said to wear down self-importance, while no rite ripens without the gatekeeper's grace. The warlike youth and the kindly gatekeeper thus guard two gates of growth: courage that slays self-love, and grace that opens every blocked road. In practice, begin undertakings by honoring the remover of blocks, and offer every promotion of ego to be disciplined into courage.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-094"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-443", title: "Tuṣṭiḥ", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is ever content. Tuṣṭi means glad contentment, first of seven names unfolding Her inner qualities. The passage says Her joy lies in giving itself: She is only the giver and delights in bestowal without need. Contentment here is fullness that overflows, not satiety that hoards. The devotee meets sufficiency as a person. Learn Her economy of joy: She spends Herself in giving yet never runs empty, and invites you to taste wealth that grows precisely by being shared. In practice, rehearse enough-ness: before asking for more, thank Her for what sustains you, and discover happiness by causing happiness in another creature today.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-094"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-444", title: "Puṣṭiḥ", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the power of nourishment. Puṣṭi means thriving fullness that fills out body and mind. The passage places Her as the second of the seven qualities, the sap that turns mere existence into health and vigor. She feeds tissues, steadies nerves, and fattens thought with clarity. Growth is grace before it is effort. Whatever in you feels thin or withered is Her summons to be filled; open the vessel of habit, and let Her sap of plenty rise within. In practice, honor nourishment deliberately: eat cleanly, rest enough, study what strengthens, and share food or encouragement so that another's fullness also grows.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-094"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-445", title: "Matiḥ", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who manifests as intelligence. Mati means discerning intellect ripened by experience when spirit shines through the person. The passage counts this as the third inner quality and notes that the still Lord too is called knowing through mastery of sacred lore. Intelligence here is not cunning but the capacity to distinguish real from seeming. She thinks within our thinking. Sharpen this gift on sacred study and honest self-review, until cleverness settles into wisdom that discerns the lasting from the glittering. In practice, train judgment daily: gather facts calmly, test motives honestly, choose the long good over the quick gain, and dedicate each clear decision to Her.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-094"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-446", title: "Dhṛtiḥ", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is fortitude. Dhṛti means steadfast courage that holds firm under weight. The passage lists it fourth among the seven qualities, the staying power required to administer vast worlds without faltering. Endurance in Her is not stubbornness but loyal support of order. Devotees borrow that backbone in trial. Borrow Her backbone when the road lengthens: plant the foot of resolve, breathe through the ache of delay, and refuse the easy desertion. In practice, convert one shaky resolve into kept promise: rise at the set hour, finish the begun task, speak truth kindly under pressure, and feel Her firmness standing within your own.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-094"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-447", title: "Śāntiḥ", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is tranquility itself. Śānti means deep peace born of sense-mastery, counted fifth among the seven qualities. The passage links it with a subtle sixteenth lunar art beyond the breath's tip where the soul is cleansed, purifying beings according to their deeds. Quiet here is active clarification, not mere absence of noise. She settles turbulence into transparency. Enter this quiet as one enters cool shade at noon; lay down the parcels of craving, and feel the dust of agitation settle upon the road. In practice, lengthen the exhale, soften gaze and jaw, withdraw one craving today, and rest attention in the hush that remains as Her own form.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-094"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-448", title: "Svastimatī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the ultimate truth. Svasti points to abiding well-being in what ultimately is, beyond passing shows and measures. The passage aligns this sixth quality with the truth of truth whose import stays hard to grasp, the ground beneath empirical fact and display. She is reality wearing benediction. All prosperities borrow from that fullness. Measure passing gains against this standard of ultimate welfare, and choose the portion that neither rust nor rumor can ever corrode. In practice, test appearances against the lasting: ask what remains when excitement fades, anchor choice in that remainder, and wish the same ultimate welfare sincerely for others.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-094"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-449", title: "Kāntiḥ", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is effulgence. Kānti means gentle radiance, here the self-shining will by which the Absolute upholds things. The passage closes the seven qualities with this sustaining glow: because it shines, the sun shines and feeds the worlds. Light is care made visible. Splendor and support are one act. Her glow asks no applause, only continuance; shine likewise in hidden duties, and let usefulness be the quiet proof of your light. In practice, become a small sun in your circle: brighten one room with order, one mind with encouragement, one body with food, reflecting Her will to sustain rather than to dazzle.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-094"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-450", title: "Nandinī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who gives delight. Nandinī names the wish-yielding cow of plenty and also the purifying river of heaven. The passage holds both senses: She grants whatever the heart rightly asks and cleanses as she gives. Delight here is holy, washing the receiver even while pleasing. Bounty and purity arrive together. Twin gifts arrive in one name, bounty and bathing: what She grants also rinses, so that fortune leaves the holder cleaner than it found him. Receive, be rinsed, and pass the stream along. In practice, receive gifts as trust rather than possession, pass some delight promptly to another, and bathe conduct daily in truthfulness so joy stays clean.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-094"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-451", title: "Vighnanāśinī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who destroys all obstacles. Vighna means block and nāśinī means destroyer, especially the bars across the road to Self-knowing. The passage is terse because the office is plain: She clears what study and effort alone cannot move. Hindrances outer and inner yield to grace invoked with sincerity. Her removal educates as well as frees. Barriers named before Her begin to thin like mist at sunrise; advance one honest step, and watch the rest dissolve under Her regard. In practice, name your chief obstacle without self-pity, take the next lawful step against it, and ask Her to dissolve the remainder, promising to use the cleared path for knowing.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-094"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-452", title: "Tejovatī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is effulgent. Tejas means piercing brilliance, close to the prior radiance yet distinct. The passage separates will from work: the earlier glow is the resolve to sustain, this fire is creation's enactment, since both making and keeping need light. From Her luminous nature lesser lights derive, while sun and moon keep station under the changeless rule. She is the laboratory as well as the lamp. Creation needs both lamp and laboratory, resolve and radiance together; supply your own small share of each whenever you build anything worthwhile. In practice, turn brightness into building: use a clear morning hour to start or repair something useful, letting inner light become outer form.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-095"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-453", title: "Trinayanā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who has the sun, moon and fire as Her three eyes. Tri means three and nayanā means eyes, naming solar, lunar, and fiery gazes. The passage, echoing the Lord's threefold seeing as perception, inference, and sacred wisdom, recalls the disciplines of hearing, reflection, and meditation by which the Self is known. Her subtler love-diagram form and the brow center of clairvoyance are also joined here, with three worship temperaments led by one guide to one goal. Keep all three gazes polished, for the sun shows facts, the moon shows moods, and fire shows what must change. In practice, test every claim three ways — see directly, reason carefully, consult the wise — before you act.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-095"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-454", title: "Lolākṣī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who has rolling eyes. Lolākṣī pictures lively, glancing eyes, read in the source as the form of holy desire in the feminine. The passage cites the teaching that righteous desire, culminating in release, is itself divine, and wonders whether Her only want is for the Lord or, through Him, for all beings, since motherhood needs holy longing to create. She is also linked with the goddess of longing among the eight mothers of human drives honored at the diagram's gate. The stotra pāda pairs it with Kāmarūpiṇī — ‘she who embodies love’ — counted within this name in the edition followed here. In practice, purify wanting into blessing.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-095"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-455", title: "Mālinī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is wearing garlands. Mālā means garland, here the fifty-one letters of sacred speech strung as ornament because She is sound's Absolute whence all syllables rise. The passage distinguishes ordered mother-of-letters from artfully scrambled rows, retells the tale of the friend who held the Lord's foot at the wedding until all fortunes were settled on her companion, and notes both a tantra and a seven-year maiden bearing this name. Letters are Her jewelry and Her weapon. Every alphabet you utter is a flower from Her string; gather them gratefully, arrange them truthfully, and offer them back unbroken. In practice, wear speech carefully: string true, kind, needful words as your daily garland and unstring gossip.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-095"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-456", title: "Haṃsinī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is not separate from hamsas (the yogins who have reached great spiritual heights). Haṃsa names both swan and the breath-mantra heard as in-breath and out-breath through the centers from dawn to dawn. The passage joins the natural gliding of swans with the poised gait praised in the hymn of beauty as practiced even in the supreme household. Great yogins are Her swans, moving cleanly between waters. Mantra rides on breathing. Ride the breath as swans ride water, gliding without splash; let each in-draw gather Her name and each release return the world to Her. In practice, link remembrance to breath for a set period: hear the twin syllables in coming and going air without strain.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-095"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-457", title: "Mātā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the mother of the universe. Mātā means mother, here without the auspicious prefix of the opening name, a deliberate non-repetition in a hymn that never repeats. The passage, noting the parallel of the unadorned and adorned Lord's names, reads this in its mantra-context as mother of sacred formulas and mother of letters. She births sound as She births worlds. Motherhood is authorship and nurture together. Treat each formula as a nursling of this Mother: shelter it from idle talk, feed it with rhythmic return, and watch it grow teeth against darkness. In practice, treat every mantra as a child of Her: receive it with clean hands, feed it with repetition, and shelter it from idle chatter.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-095"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-458", title: "Malayācalavāsinī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who resides in the malaya mountain. Malaya names the fragrant southern range rich in sandal, also the gracious land where She is honored as the Blessed Lady, famed like the garden of the chief of bright powers. The passage draws a subtler scent: mantras exhale a natural fragrance as sandalwoods breathe perfume, so place figures power. Dwelling means haunting with presence. Fragrance follows faithfulness as scent follows sandalwood; keep one seat, one hour, one word, and the air around your life will sweeten. In practice, make your own seat fragrant by quiet repetition: keep one corner clean, sit at one hour, and let steadiness perfume the household.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-095"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-459", title: "Sumukhī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who has a beautiful face. Su means fair and mukhī means faced, and face is taken as the mirror of wisdom. The passage cites the ancient praise of a shining countenance as proof of knowing, the refuge sought in the luminous revealer of Self-knowledge, and the matching Vaiṣṇava title. Spiritual advance is said to brighten the features with untainted purity incapable of meanness. Initiation should be sought from such lit faces, not from mere ritualists. A face kept toward the light cannot help reflecting it; turn yours daily to the source until others read calm there. In practice, polish the mirror: speak truth, digest food and news moderately, and let morning quiet lend your face its steadiness.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-096"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-460", title: "Nalinī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She whose body is soft and beautiful like lotus petals. Nalinī means lotus-like, extending the earlier enthronement on the lotus seat into identity with the flower itself. The passage admires Her sculpted grace through this water-born emblem of unfolding purity, and adds that the heavenly river of purification also bears this name. Softness here is strength without thorn. Petals suggest graded opening. Unfold as the lotus unfolds, petal by patient petal, rooted in mud yet unsoiled, opening wider to the same sun that raised you. In practice, soften without collapsing: be pliant in manner, firm in principle, and open one more petal of patience toward a difficult person today.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-096"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-461", title: "Subhrūḥ", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who has beautiful eyebrows. Su means lovely and bhrū means brows, already likened to festive festoons. The passage recalls the hymn that sees in Her slightly knit brows and bee-like eyes the lover's bow strung across by the Lord to rout the devotee's fear. Architecture of the face becomes artillery of mercy. Even seeming sternness protects. Let your expressions become small guardians of the fearful: knit care where danger threatens, and arch encouragement where hearts begin to droop. In practice, bend your own brow-work toward shelter: knit attention to guard the fearful, lift gloom with a reassuring glance, and let expression become a small bow for others.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-096"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-462", title: "Śobhanā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is always radiant. Śobhanā means splendid with welfare, the point where the bards confess defeat before divine beauty. The passage says comparisons with human objects fail, so the hymn crowns delineation with sheer auspicious loveliness, wherein charm, virtue, prosperity, and moral good coincide. Radiance is goodness shining. When comparisons fail, character remains; polish conduct until it gleams, and you will carry Her kind of beauty that no season wrinkles and no rival dims. In practice, stop grading yourself against passing models; instead brighten conduct until character itself becomes ornament, and let one act of quiet generosity supply today's splendor.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-096"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-463", title: "Suranāyikā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the leader of the gods. Sura means bright divinity and nāyikā means leader, setting Her above the high offices of projection, maintenance, and withdrawal as well as their luminous helpers. The passage distinguishes commanding gods from admirable subordinates: She assigns the cosmic work while others execute portions. Headship is responsibility, not rivalry. Rank in Her order means burden borne for others; accept the small scepters of home and work as commissions from Her throne. In practice, lead whatever small host you head — family, team, or senses — by apportioning clear duties, honoring competence, and answering yourself for the outcome.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-096"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-464", title: "Kālakaṇṭhī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the wife of shiva. Kāla can mean the dark poison and kaṇṭhī the throated lady, wedding Her to the blue-throated Lord who drank the churned venom while She stayed His swallowing. The passage also hears kala as soft sound, praising Her sweet voice celebrated earlier, and notes a fierce emanation with a like name sent against demons. Consortship holds both rescue and music. Stand at loved throats in hours of poison, checking harm midway with timely counsel and a voice made soft as balm. In practice, stand at the throat of crisis for those you love: check the poison mid-way with timely counsel, and soften your own voice into medicine.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-096"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-465", title: "Kāntimatī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is radiant. Kānti is splendor and matī is gifted with mind, so the name can be heard as radiant intelligence. The passage refers back to the earlier effulgence, sharpening it: not only does light sustain, but understanding shines. Thought itself glows when near its source. Beauty and brains are one ray. Polish the mind like a lamp-glass, wiping soot of hurry and grudge, until the flame of understanding burns clearly through it. In practice, feed intellect with light-giving study and company, rest it with sleep and silence, and spend its brightness in explaining something hard kindly to someone who needs it.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-096"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-466", title: "Kṣobhiṇī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who creates upheaval in the mind. Kṣobha means tremor or stirring, here the creative throb She wakes in stillness so that souls wed nature and worlds begin. The passage traces the unfolding from subtle to gross as dormant deeds ripen, calling the pulse the dynamic teaching of tremor. Agitation is thus beginning, not breakdown. Without Her nudge nothing stirs toward birth. Do not drug holy restlessness with distraction; shape its tremor with vow, study, and service until shaking settles into making. In practice, when holy restlessness shakes routine, do not drug it with distraction; shape it with vow, study, and service until tremor becomes creation.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-096"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-467", title: "Sūkṣmarūpiṇī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who has a form that is too subtle to be perceived by the sense organs. Sūkṣma means finer than fine, subtler than the smallest yet vaster than the vast. The passage orders Her three bodies: ponderable image for meditation, diagram of desire for inward worship, and coiled energy as subtlest ground. Ancient verses on the atomic Self and the matching Vaiṣṇava title are cited, with the awestruck confession of unbearable brilliance and the mind-made fire-offering in the root. Withdraw from gross noise to breath, from breath to watcher, until fineness itself dissolves into the formless ground. In practice, refine perception by withdrawing from gross noise daily, then attending to breath, then to the attender.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-096"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-468", title: "Vajreśvarī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is vajreśvarī, the sixth daily deity. Vajra means diamond-thunder and īśvarī means sovereign lady, enthroned in the northern seat at the throat wheel and again in the innermost triangle as great sovereign of wisdom, dream, and knowing power. The passage warns that ritual without grasping these cosmic offices misses realization, since each deity enacts one act of the ground crowned at the center-point by stillness and reflection. A hidden river of swan-music and Indra's thunder-gift adorn Her lore. One daily rhythm kept with understanding outweighs scattered rites; choose your hour, learn its meaning, and let repetition ripen into realization. In practice, honor one daily rhythm faithfully, studying its meaning until rite becomes insight.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-097"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-469", title: "Vāmadevī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the wife of vāma deva (shiva). Vāma means left as well as lovely, so She is the left-half lady of the gracious five-faced Lord whose upper face rules ether. The passage depicts that noble red form in red garb and garlands, gives His absolving mantra, and reads their joint half-male half-female figure as Her occupying His left, Her rose hue warming His crystal white. Left-hand rites also gather under Her name, though scripture tests them. Wed firm resolve with a gentle left hand that feeds, forgives, and adorns whatever strength has rightly won. In practice, wed strength with grace: let firm resolve keep a gentle left hand that feeds, forgives, and adorns.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-097"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-470", title: "Vayo'avasthāvivarjitā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is exempt from changes due to age (time). Vayas means age and avasthā means stage, both denied by vivarjitā, utterly excluded. The passage states plainly that this is the changeless mark of the ground which does not pass through childhood, youth, and decay. Time acts within Her, not upon Her. Devotees meet constancy itself. Serve the aging frame with reverence while refusing its calendar as your identity; the tenant abides though the tenement weathers. In practice, anchor identity in what does not age — awareness, kindness, truth — while serving the aging body dutifully without confusing yourself with its calendar.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-097"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-471", title: "Siddheśvari", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the goddess worshipped by spiritual adepts. Siddhas are perfected ones bearing eight extraordinary capacities won through the ascending discipline, and īśvarī is their sovereign. The passage pictures the still pole waiting motionless at the crown while She as coiled power rises to meet Him, adored by practitioners in that moving, fiery shape. Mastery bows to Mother. Court capacities only as servants of compassion; let each small power earned kneel promptly to relieve some creature's ache. In practice, seek powers only as by-products: keep vows, breath, and mantra clean, revere the ascent rather than display, and let any capacity ripen into service.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-097"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-472", title: "Siddhavidyā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is in the form of siddhavidyā, the fifteen-syllabled mantra. Vidyā is the goddess-formula as mantra is the god-formula, and siddha means already proven and safe. The passage explains testing of ordinary formulas by teachers, the soul-syllable that can be set before, after, or around a formula to sweeten it, and the exemption of the fifteenfold one from such trial and timing, since the planets themselves serve Her. Potency is maternal reliability. Receive sacred formula solely from proven hands, guard its hours and diet of conduct, and repeat it as homage rather than bargain. In practice, receive mantra only from a tested guide, guard its discipline, and repeat it with devotion rather than bargaining.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-097"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-473", title: "Siddhamātā", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is the mother of siddhas. Mātā means mother, and even renouncers who quit all bonds keep this one exception. The passage recalls Her supreme motherhood hymned at opening and middle, picturing deep absorbers who forgo food for seasons, living linked to the cosmos through the coiled channel, calling Her simply Mother. She guards them as a mother guards children. Simplify wants, keep silent company with stillness and power each dawn, and trust Her nursing through every dry or dark passage. In practice, simplify wants, keep silent company with the pair of stillness and power daily, and trust Her nursing care through dry or dark stages.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-097"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-474", title: "Yaśasvinī", category: "Aiśvarya", summary: "She who is of unequalled renown. Yaśas means glory that spreads, and the name crowns Her manifold competence by which, once projected, She governs the whole without further interference. The passage cites the hymn of the immeasurable whose upper, middle, and across no mind grasps, named great glory itself. Fame here is cosmic efficacy recognized. The section then turns toward the wheel-guardians who serve Her ascent. Work so well and quietly that praise becomes unnecessary; true glory, like true fragrance, announces itself without any herald. In practice, let your good name grow the same way: do needed work so well and quietly that others need not be asked to praise it.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-097"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-475", title: "Viśuddhicakranilayā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who resides in the vishuddhi chakra. Viśuddhi means thorough purification, the throat wheel of smoky purple with sixteen vowel-petals and the white ether-seed borne on a white elephant. The passage opens the sixty-name wheel sequence out of bodily order, beginning where speech issues as uttered word, and explains its frame: each wheel is named first and its guardian only later, so single names stay incomplete without their set. Here the guardian is the single-faced keeper. Begin the inward climb where voice is born; cleanse talk at its throat-spring, and the higher centers will welcome a pilgrim already truthful. In practice, purify speech at its throat source: speak less, speak true, and sound the sacred syllable clearly.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-098"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-476", title: "Āraktavarṇā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is of slightly red (rosy) complexion. Ārakta means lightly reddened and varṇā means color, describing the throat-wheel guardian's mild rose hue. The passage is brief because color here is character: warmth tempering ether's openness, vigor inside clarity. Rosy suggests dawn rather than blaze. A tender tint upon vast ether teaches proportion: clarity need not chill, nor warmth confuse. Keep frankness rosy with kindness, correction gentle with hope, and devotion quietly aglow like first light upon snow. In practice, tint your own clarity with warmth: correct error without coldness, keep frank speech kind, and let a little healthy color of feeling show in devotion.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-098"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-477", title: "Trilocanā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who has three eyes. Tri means three and locanā means eyes, marking the throat guardian as sharing the triple gaze of the high gods. The passage notes this plainly, since threefold seeing — outward, inward, and transcendent — belongs even to gatekeepers of speech. Vision guards utterance. Threefold sight belongs even at the gate of speech: fact, feeling, and the spark beyond both. Look outward accurately, inward honestly, and upward humbly before a single word is weighed and released. In practice, look thrice before speaking: at fact, at motive, and at consequence, and let the third eye of conscience keep the other two honest.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-098"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-478", title: "Khaṭvāṅgādipraharaṇā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is armed with a club and other weapons. Khaṭvāṅga names the staff crowned with a skull, with other arms implied. The passage assigns this stark club to the throat guardian as her emblem: speech-power can shatter delusion and defend the path. The skull finial reminds that words outlast speakers. The stark staff with its skull-crown warns that words outlive their speakers and can crush delusion or bruise hearts. Wield truth as surgeons wield the knife, cutting disease while sparing the patient. In practice, arm your own speech with disciplined study and vow, using sharp truth to cut confusion while refusing to wound persons with mockery or spite.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-098"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-479", title: "Vadanaikasamanvitā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who possesses only one face. Vadana means face and eka means one, so the throat keeper looks single-faced. The passage explains the ordering key of this whole wheel-sequence: guardians are ranked by faces from one upward, and this first wheel's single face suits the ether-principle's simplicity. Unity of face figures unity of voice. One face for thought, word, and deed gives speech the weight of integrity. When inner estimate matches outer utterance, even simple sentences carry the quiet authority of a vow kept whole. In practice, keep one face in speech: let inner thought, private word, and public statement agree, so utterance carries the force of integrity.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-098"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-480", title: "Pāyasānnapriyā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is especially fond of sweet rice. Pāyasa-anna is rice or grain simmered with milk and sugar, the festive sweet offered to bright powers. The passage says the throat guardian loves this soft, white, nourishing food, matching her vowel-wheel of speech and milk-like clarity. Offering mirrors organ: sweetness for the voice. Sweetness offered upward returns as sweetness of tongue; feed the voice with clean food, clean reading, and clean company, until talk itself becomes an oblation poured gently into listening ears. In practice, sweeten speech-offerings literally and figuratively: share clean, nourishing food on holy days and feed conversation with praise that is true, sparing, and timely.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-099"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-481", title: "Tvaksthā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is the deity of the organ of touch (skin). Tvak means skin and sthā means abiding in, so the throat guardian governs the outermost sheath and its feeling. The passage finds design in beginning the wheel-ascent here: from the farthest frontier of touch inward toward blood, flesh, and subtler grounds. Boundary becomes doorway. The frontier sense deserves a guardian's honor, for through touch the world first knocks. Meet contact mindfully, grasping little, blessing much, and dedicating sensation to discernment. In practice, guard the frontier mindfully: dress cleanly, touch gently, notice contact without grasping, and dedicate the sense of feel to discerning what truly nourishes.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-099"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-482", title: "Paśulokabhayaṅkarī", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who fills with fear the mortal beings bound by worldly existence. Paśu means the bound beast, the soul that mistakes otherness for reality, and bhayaṅkarī means fear-arousing. The passage contrasts offices: the supreme Mother loosens bonds while this guardian startles the bound, citing teachings that separative worship keeps one cattle-like to bright powers and that fear springs from sensing a second. Terror can school toward unity when rightly read. Let wholesome dread become a teacher pointing home: wherever otherness frightens, rebuild unity with confession, restitution, and steady remembrance of the one Self. In practice, heed wholesome fear as signal: where division frightens, repair it with truth, humility, and remembrance.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-099"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-483", title: "Amṛtādimahāśaktisaṃvṛtā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is surrounded by amṛtā and other shakti deities. Saṃvṛtā means encircled, here by sixteen great powers, each ruling one petal and vowel from the nectar-letter onward. The passage stresses mahā, great, marking them as potent though subordinate to their mistress of the throat wheel. Retinue images articulation: each sound has its guardian intelligence. Mastery grows petal by petal when each detail receives its dues; learn letters, duties, and notes singly with reverence until the whole blooms around its center. In practice, honor detail within wholes: learn letters, notes, or duties one by one with their presiding care, building mastery petal by petal around a steady center.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-099"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-484", title: "Ḍākinīśvarī", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is the Ḍākinī deity. The name discloses the veiled subject of ten names: the sovereign guardian of the throat wheel. The passage closes the first wheel-set by naming her outright, binding residence, color, eyes, weapon, face, food, tissue, terror, and retinue into one person. Sequence becomes portrait. Gather studied parts into a living whole before moving on; salute this keeper of utterance, close the first wheel with gratitude, and turn inward toward the heart. In practice, complete what you study the same way: after noting parts, gather them into a living whole, salute the guardian intelligence of speech, and proceed inward to the next center.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-099"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-485", title: "Anāhatābjanilayā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who resides in the anāhata lotus in the heart. Anāhata means unstruck, the heart wheel where sound rises without friction as the ground of sacred word. The passage pictures a dull-red twelve-petal lotus with consonants, hexagonal heart-space, and protective air-seed, housing both the thumb-sized inner person of the old texts and a smaller eight-petal seat of the chosen deity, under a guardian whose name varies across manuals. Each passage of the supreme power blesses the soul and thins ripening deeds. Lay attention upon the quiet heart lotus, and listen beneath breath for the hum that no friction ever made. In practice, lay hand on heart in silence and listen for the unstruck hum beneath breath.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-100"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-486", title: "Śyāmābhā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is black in complexion. Śyāma means dark green-black and ābhā means sheen, opening the heart guardian's portrait as youthful, darkly radiant, sixteen in ageless vigor. The passage gives color and age together: depth that absorbs, youth that renews. Darkness here shelters life like rain-cloud. Honor darkness as Her sheltering robe: night, rest, and unknowing are not refusals but womb-times. What ripens in shadow, seed, child, or insight, emerges in season wearing Her deep and steady hue. In practice, honor the dark half of experience — night, rest, unknowing — as Her complexion too, trusting that hidden growth ripens under its cover.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-100"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-487", title: "Vadanadvayā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who has two faces. Dvaya means pair, so the heart guardian looks two ways. The passage reads this through the faces-ladder: single-faced throat below in the telling order, double-faced heart now, more above. Number marks ascent in capacity. Two faces can watch inner and outer at once. Two faces turn together here, one toward duty and one toward witness. Act in the world with careful hands while a second gaze rests upon the actor, keeping traffic between outer work and inner silence always open. In practice, train double attention: keep one face on the task and one on the witness, acting carefully while remembering who acts.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-100"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-488", title: "Daṃṣṭrojjvalā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who has shining tusks. Daṃṣṭra means great tooth or tusk and ujjvalā means blazing, giving the heart guardian a wild-boar fierceness. The passage notes terror as function: tenderness of heart needs a guard that frightens intruders like cruelty and lust. Brightness keeps fear clean rather than murky. Fierce guardianship befits a tender treasure: the heart needs bright tusks against intruders like cruelty, envy, and lust. Flash a clean refusal where softness would be devoured, and keep compassion safely guarded from wolves. In practice, let compassion grow tusks: refuse entry to habits that devour the heart, and flash a firm, shining no where gentleness would be exploited.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-100"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-489", title: "Akṣamālādidharā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is wearing garlands of rudrākṣa beads and other things. Akṣamālā is the rosary, here fifty-one beads for the letters, apt for the wheel of unstruck sound borne on air. The passage cites the rosary lore: gems and metals strung on precious thread, consecrated by rule, quicken every formula, and all counters should be honored as sacred. Sound and counting wed. Count without display and store the string with honor; a consecrated counter, truly used, becomes a ladder with fifty-one rungs toward silence. In practice, choose one clean rosary, consecrate it by resolve and care, count without display, and store it respectfully as a tool of remembrance.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-100"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-490", title: "Rudhirasaṃsthitā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who presides over the blood in the bodies of living beings. Rudhira means blood and saṃsthitā means established over, assigning the heart guardian the tissue next beneath skin. The passage finds order in this: outer covering first, then life-fluid, matching the wheel-sequence from throat to heart. Blood figures courage and kinship. Build worthy blood with temperate fuel, clean grain, restful nights, and governed temper, and spend its vigor in errands of mercy rather than quarrels. In practice, honor blood by what builds it — wholesome food, sleep, restraint from stimulants and rage — and by giving help where life-fluid is needed, in charity or care.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-100"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-491", title: "Kālarātryādiśaktyaughavṛtā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is surrounded by kālarātri and other shaktis. Vṛtā means girt by a flood of powers led by the dark night goddess born of inertia, three-eyed, sun-bright, black-robed, four-armed with emblems and blessings, served by bright ones. The passage says her formula routs foes, her shape is a fierce Mother's, her dream-visit foretells passing, and her name also marks the ripe age when rule-books release their hold. Retinue as night-watch. Ring the heart with night-watch powers: a fixed vow, studied pages, and prayerful friends who keep vigil when shadows lengthen around resolve. In practice, post brave guards round the heart: vow, study, and prayerful friends that circle you when darkness gathers.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-101"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-492", title: "Snigdhaudanapriyā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is fond of food offerings containing ghee, oil and other substances containing fats. Snigdha means unctuous and odana means cooked rice, a soft oily dish that builds good blood. The passage links offering to office with homely logic: the blood-guardian loves what makes rich blood. Worship feeds physiology transfigured. Kitchen and shrine meet in this teaching, for both sustain the labor of the heart; cook and offer mindfully, eat gratefully, and waste neither morsel nor moment. In practice, offer and eat fats wisely and moderately — clean ghee, timely meals, no excess — remembering that kitchen and shrine both sustain the heart's work.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-101"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-493", title: "Mahāvīrendravaradā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who bestows boons on great warriors. Vīra means brave energies, the senses, and their lord is who masters them into the rapture of I-awareness across waking, dream, and sleep toward the fourth lucidity and beyond. The passage fits gifts to emblems: eloquence for the rosaried, airy, sounding guardian, and blessings for those who outgrow I-versus-this. True soldiers conquer within. Drill one sense-soldier each day into obedience, tongue from gossip, eye from envy, ear from flattery, and true eloquence will arrive as a medal of self-rule. In practice, drill one sense-soldier daily — tongue, eye, or ear under orders — and ask Her to turn discipline into clear, kindly speech.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-101"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-494", title: "Rākiṇyambāsvarūpiṇī", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is in the form of the rākiṇi deity. Svarūpiṇī means assuming the very form, here closing the ten-name heart set in one disclosure. The passage gathers the preceding nine marks into their bearer, as with the throat guardian before. Naming seals meditation. Review the whole heart-set as one portrait, place, hue, faces, tusks, beads, blood, ring, food, and boon, then bow and ready the lamp for the fire-center below. In practice, review the heart wheel whole — place, color, faces, tusks, rosary, blood, retinue, food, and boon — then bow to its motherly keeper and ready attention for the fire center below.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-101"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-495", title: "Maṇipūrābjanilayā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who resides in the ten-petaled lotus in the maṇipūraka chakra. Maṇipūra means city of gems, the navel wheel of ten consonant-petals around a red inverted triangle marked thrice with the auspicious cross, holding the fiery seed of twelve-sun force that empowers great formulas and the crafting of personal seed-sounds. The passage notes digestion's inner fire and desire's heat dwell here, creative and ruinous by combination, and that full awakening seldom sinks below this station. Tend the navel fire with temperate fuel and deep breathing, turning animal heat into steady will that digests both food and experience cleanly. In practice, tend belly-fire: eat temperately, breathe deeply into the navel, and channel warmth into work.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-102"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-496", title: "Vadanatrayasaṃyutā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who has three faces. Traya means triad, so the navel guardian bears three countenances. The passage places her third in the faces-ladder of the telling, matching her station as third wheel expounded. Three faces survey three worlds of function. Three faces survey impulse from every side, and haste cannot survive such inspection. Ask where a drive was born, what road it travels, and what harvest it promises before command is given to act. In practice, check each fiery impulse three ways — origin, means, and end — letting triple vision cool haste into rightly aimed resolve before you speak or spend.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-102"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-497", title: "Vajrādikāyudhopetā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who holds the vajra (lightning bolt) and other weapons. Upetā means endowed with, here thunderbolt and power-lance in two of four arms, the others showing bounty and fearlessness. The passage reads the set plainly: striking force with sheltering grace, fitting the fire wheel that both cooks food and burns bonds. Might serves mercy. Balance the four hands of power: two upholding standards and boundaries firmly, two bestowing bounty and protection freely, never one pair marching without the other. In practice, keep your own four hands balanced: two for firm boundaries and standards, two for giving and reassuring, never brandishing one pair without the other.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-102"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-498", title: "Ḍāmaryādibhirāvṛtā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is surrounded by Ḍāmarī and other attending deities. Āvṛtā means encompassed, here by ten attendants matching ten petals, foremost the tumultuous one. The passage is spare because pattern now teaches: each wheel-mistress stands ringed by petal-powers. Company multiplies function, for every petal of effort deserves its angel. Lonely resolve seldom holds its post through siege; station ten small allies, set hours, clean tools, true companions, and brief prayers, around your center until habit itself stands guard. In practice, surround your own center with ten small helps — fixed hours, clean tools, true friends, brief prayers — so resolve is never left single against habit.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-102"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-499", title: "Raktavarṇā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who is red in complexion. Rakta means blood-red and varṇā means hue, clothing the navel guardian in fire's own color. The passage states this without gloss, since element declares itself: heat, digestion, and drive show as red. Color becomes instruction. Read your reds with honesty each evening: bless the warm glow of work and worship well spent, and cool the fever-flush of anger with water, breath, and deliberate pause. In practice, read your own reds: welcome the warm red of energy rightly used in work and worship, and cool the inflamed red of anger or greed with water, breath, and pause.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-103"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-500", title: "Māṃsaniṣṭhā", category: "Cakra-yoginīs", summary: "She who presides over the flesh in living beings. Māṃsa means muscle-flesh and niṣṭhā means established over, giving the navel guardian the tissue beneath skin and blood. The passage notes the ladder-logic again: coverings descend inward as wheels descend in telling, flesh following outer layers. Embodiment deepens step by step. Treat flesh as a field held in trust from Her: till it with movement, enrich it with wholesome food, rest it fallow in sleep, and harvest its strength for neighbors in need. In practice, honor flesh as trust: feed it steadily, exercise it honestly, rest it fully, and offer its strength for labor that feeds or heals others.", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-103"] },
]
